{"concept": "complexification", "normalized_concept": "complexification", "status": "grounded", "source": "wikidata", "id": "Q445569", "title": "complexification", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q445569", "description": "topic in mathematics", "aliases": [], "accepted_urls": ["http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q445569"], "candidate_urls": ["http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q105647346", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q113187770", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q114021984", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q125289489", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q126463775", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q128279607", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q129281555", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q17005573", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q445569"], "score": 0.95, "confidence": "llm_page_judged", "reason": "Wikipedia intro explicitly defines the complexification of a real vector space as a complex vector space; matches the sentence's meaning.", "candidates": [{"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q445569", "title": "complexification", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q445569", "description": "topic in mathematics", "aliases": [], "type_ids": ["Q976981"], "type_labels": ["formula"], "page_title": "Complexification", "page_extract": "In mathematics, the complexification of a vector space V over the field of real numbers (a "real vector space") yields a vector space VC over the complex number field, obtained by formally extending the scaling of vectors by real numbers to include their scaling ("multiplication") by complex numbers. Any basis for V (a space over the real numbers) may also serve as a basis for VC over the complex numbers.", "page_categories": ["Articles with short description", "CS1: long volume value", "Complex manifolds", "Short description matches Wikidata", "Vector spaces"], "score": 0.95, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "accept", "llm_confidence": 0.95, "llm_reason": "Wikipedia intro explicitly defines the complexification of a real vector space as a complex vector space; matches the sentence's meaning.", "is_math_concept": true, "is_same_sense_as_context": true}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q17005573", "title": "Complexification", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q17005573", "description": "", "aliases": ["universal complexification"], "type_ids": ["Q976981"], "type_labels": ["formula"], "page_title": "Complexification (Lie group)", "page_extract": "In mathematics, the complexification or universal complexification of a real Lie group is given by a continuous homomorphism of the group into a complex Lie group with the universal property that every continuous homomorphism of the original group into another complex Lie group extends compatibly to a complex analytic homomorphism between the complex Lie groups. The complexification, which always exists, is unique up to unique isomorphism. Its Lie algebra is a quotient of the complexification of the Lie algebra of the original group. They are isomorphic if the original group has a quotient by a discrete normal subgroup which is linear.
For compact Lie groups, the complexification, sometimes called the Chevalley complexification after Claude Chevalley, can be defined as the group of complex characters of the Hopf algebra of representative functions, i.e. the matrix coefficients of finite-dimensional representations of the group. In any finite-dimensional faithful unitary representation of the compact group it can be realized concretely as a closed subgroup of the complex general linear group. It consists of operators with polar decomposition g = u • exp iX, where u is a unitary operator in the compact group and X is a skew-adjoint operator in its Lie algebra. In this case the complexification is a complex algebraic group and its Lie algebra is the complexification of the Lie algebra of the compact Lie group.

", "page_categories": ["Algebraic groups", "Articles with short description", "CS1 Russian-language sources (ru)", "Lie algebras", "Lie groups", "Representation theory", "Short description with empty Wikidata description"], "score": 0.0, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "reject", "llm_confidence": 0.9, "llm_reason": "Mathematical concept (complexification of a Lie group) but a different sense (Lie group complexification), not the vector-space complexification in the sentence.", "is_math_concept": true, "is_same_sense_as_context": false}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q125289489", "title": "Complexification of gauge theories", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q125289489", "description": "scientific article published in 1996", "aliases": [], "type_ids": ["Q13442814"], "type_labels": ["scholarly article"], "page_title": "", "page_extract": "", "page_categories": [], "score": 0.0, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "reject", "llm_confidence": 0.8, "llm_reason": "Scholarly article about gauge theories, not a general definition of the vector-space complexification.", "is_math_concept": false, "is_same_sense_as_context": false}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q114021984", "title": "Complexification of the projective and injective tensor products", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q114021984", "description": "scientific article published in 2008", "aliases": [], "type_ids": ["Q13442814"], "type_labels": ["scholarly article"], "page_title": "", "page_extract": "", "page_categories": [], "score": 0.0, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "reject", "llm_confidence": 0.8, "llm_reason": "Scholarly article; not a general definition page for the mathematical concept in the sentence.", "is_math_concept": false, "is_same_sense_as_context": false}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q113187770", "title": "Complexification of eukaryote phenotype: Adaptive immuno-cognitive systems as unique Gödelian block chain distributed ledger", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/enti