", "page_categories": ["All articles needing additional references", "Articles needing additional references from July 2016", "Articles with short description", "Mathematical terminology", "Short description is different from Wikidata"], "score": 0.95, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "accept", "llm_confidence": 0.95, "llm_reason": "The Wikipedia article 'Connectedness' defines the mathematical notion of connectedness and connected components, which matches the usage in the Lie group identity-component context.", "is_math_concept": true, "is_same_sense_as_context": true}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q7551008", "title": "social connectedness", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7551008", "description": "term in psychology referring to the experience of feeling close and connected to others", "aliases": ["connectedness", "social connection"], "type_ids": ["Q205555"], "type_labels": ["feeling"], "page_title": "Social connection", "page_extract": "Social connection is the experience of feeling close and connected to others. It involves feeling loved, cared for, and valued, and forms the basis of interpersonal relationships."Connection is the energy that exists between people when they feel seen, heard and valued; when they can give and receive without judgement; and when they derive sustenance and strength from the relationship." —Brené Brown, Professor of social work at the University of HoustonIncreasingly, social connection is understood as a core human need, and the desire to connect as a fundamental drive. It is crucial to development; without it, social animals experience distress and face severe developmental consequences. In humans, one of the most social species, social connection is essential to nearly every aspect of health and well-being. Lack of connection, or loneliness, has been linked to inflammation, accelerated aging and cardiovascular health risk, suicide, and all-cause mortality.
Feeling socially connected depends on the quality and number of meaningful relationships one has with family, friends, and acquaintances. Going beyond the individual level, it also involves a feeling of connecting to a larger community. Connectedness on a community level has profound benefits for both individuals and society.", "page_categories": ["Articles citing publications with errata", "Articles with short description", "CS1 errors: ISBN date", "Emotion", "Interpersonal relationships", "Short description matches Wikidata", "Social psychology concepts"], "score": 0.0, "match_type": "alias", "llm_decision": "reject", "llm_confidence": 0.95, "llm_reason": "This is a psychology/social term ('social connectedness'), not the mathematical concept.", "is_math_concept": false, "is_same_sense_as_context": false}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q5161416", "title": "Connectedness to nature scale", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5161416", "description": "", "aliases": [], "type_ids": [], "type_labels": [], "page_title": "Connectedness to nature scale", "page_extract": "The connectedness to nature scale (CNS) is a measure of individuals' trait levels of feeling emotionally connected to the natural world in the realm of social and environmental psychology. The CNS was “designed to tap an individual’s affective, experiential connection to nature.” The concept of connectedness to nature signifies the relationship between an individual and the environment. In brief, the scale intends to measure the level to which an individual feels connected with the natural world. Mayer and Frantz describe the CNS as a reliable, single-factored, multi-item scale that is easy to administer.", "page_categories": ["Emotion"], "score": 0.0, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "reject", "llm_confidence": 0.9, "llm_reason": "A psychological measurement scale ('connectedness to nature'), not the mathematical definition.", "is_math_concept": false, "is_same_sense_as_context": false}, {"sour