{"concept": "harmonic polynomial", "normalized_concept": "harmonic polynomial", "status": "grounded", "source": "wikidata", "id": "Q4133834", "title": "harmonic polynomial", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4133834", "description": "multivariate polynomial p over a field such that the Laplacian of p is zero", "aliases": [], "accepted_urls": ["http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4133834"], "candidate_urls": ["http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127177375", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127811115", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4133834"], "score": 0.95, "confidence": "llm_page_judged", "reason": "Wikidata/Wikipedia page defines 'harmonic polynomial' as a polynomial whose Laplacian is zero, matching the mathematical concept in the sentence.", "candidates": [{"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q4133834", "title": "harmonic polynomial", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4133834", "description": "multivariate polynomial p over a field such that the Laplacian of p is zero", "aliases": [], "type_ids": ["Q1244652", "Q599027"], "type_labels": ["polynomial function", "harmonic function"], "page_title": "Harmonic polynomial", "page_extract": "In mathematics, a polynomial 



        p


    {\displaystyle p}

 whose Laplacian is zero is termed a harmonic polynomial.
The harmonic polynomials form a subspace of the vector space of polynomials over the given field. In fact, they form a graded subspace. For the real field (




          R



    {\displaystyle \mathbb {R} }

), the harmonic polynomials are important in mathematical physics.
The Laplacian is the sum of second-order partial derivatives with respect to each of the variables, and is an invariant differential operator under the action of the orthogonal group via the group of rotations.
