Many of the results of finite group representation theory are proved by averaging over the group. For compact groups, modifications of these proofs yields similar results by averaging with respect to the normalized Haar integral. In the general locally compact setting, such techniques need not hold. The resulting theory is a central part of harmonic analysis. The representation theory for locally compact abelian groups is described by Pontryagin duality.", "page_categories": ["All articles lacking in-text citations", "Articles lacking in-text citations from March 2011", "Articles with short description", "CS1 French-language sources (fr)", "CS1 errors: ISBN date", "Pages displaying short descriptions of redirect targets via Module:Annotated link", "Short description is different from Wikidata", "Topological groups"], "score": 0.95, "match_type": "alias", "llm_decision": "accept", "llm_confidence": 0.95, "llm_reason": "Wikidata/Wikipedia page titled 'Locally compact group' explicitly defines the topological group whose underlying topology is locally compact and Hausdorff; matches the sentence definition.", "is_math_concept": true, "is_same_sense_as_context": true}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q137733534", "title": "locally compact group", "url": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q137733534", "description": "topological group whose points admit compact neighborhoods", "aliases": ["locally compact topological group"], "type_ids": ["Q1046291"], "type_labels": ["topological group"], "page_title": "", "page_extract": "", "page_categories": [], "score": 0.0, "match_type": "label", "llm_decision": "accept", "llm_confidence": 0.9, "llm_reason": "Explicitly describes a topological group with compact neighborhoods (locally compact); matches the mathematical concept in the sentence.", "is_math_concept": true, "is_same_sense_as_context": true}, {"source": "wikidata", "id": "Q129099907", "title": "Locally compact groups with totally disconnected space of subgroups", "url": "http: